      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright (c) 2005 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Th2 cytokines play pivotal roles in allergic inflammation, including eosinophilia, and their actions are antagonized by Th1 cytokines, conferring them therapeutic potential."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In this study, we examined the ability of a number of cytokines to suppress the activation of eosinophils that function as effector cells for allergic airway diseases."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Interleukin (IL)-5, IL-6, and tumor necrosis factor (TNF) induced an eosinophil shape change, whereas interferon (IFN)-gamma significantly inhibited the shape change. Other cytokines, including IL-1beta, IL-4, IL-10 and IL-13, had little or only slightly enhancing or reducing effects on the shape change. We further analyzed the IFN-gamma effect, showing that pretreatment with IFN-gamma strongly suppressed IL-5-induced eosinophil shape change, and cycloheximide (CHX) abrogated the suppression by IFN-gamma, suggesting that new protein synthesis is required for the inhibitory effect by this cytokine. In agreement with these results, IFN-gamma blocked the eosinophil migration and ERK phophorylation induced by IL-5, and the addition of CHX restored eosinophil chemotaxis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Collectively, IFN-gamma may attenuate eosinophilic inflammation by directly negating eosinophil mobilization."
          }
        ]
      },
